      <!-- Heading 1: Understanding Paris Geography -->
      <h2>1. Understanding the Parisian "Escargot": Left Bank vs Right Bank</h2>

      <p>Before selecting a hotel, every traveler must grasp the fundamental divide that has defined Parisian culture for a millennium: <strong>The Seine River</strong>, which cleaves the city into the <strong>Right Bank (Rive Droite)</strong> to the north and the <strong>Left Bank (Rive Gauche)</strong> to the south.</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>The Right Bank (Rive Droite):</strong> The historic domain of commerce, high finance, theater, luxury shopping, and royal palaces. Arrondissements include the 1st (Louvre), 2nd (Bourse), 3rd &amp; 4th (Le Marais), 8th (Champs-Élysées), 9th (Opéra Garnier), and the trendy 10th/11th. It is energetic, vibrant, trendsetting, and dense with dining and shopping.</li>
        <li><strong>The Left Bank (Rive Gauche):</strong> The intellectual and bohemian heart of Paris, dominated by university academies (Sorbonne), historic publishing houses, artist studios, and aristocratic private mansions (<em>hôtels particuliers</em>). Arrondissements include the 5th (Latin Quarter), 6th (Saint-Germain), and 7th (Eiffel Tower). It is quieter, more romantic, exceptionally safe, and quintessential "old-world" Paris.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="roam-callout-ai">
        <strong>💡 First-Timer Rule of Thumb:</strong> If you love vibrant nightlife, browsing fashion boutiques, visiting contemporary art galleries, and eating late in lively wine bistros, book the <strong>Right Bank</strong>. If you dream of sitting in a quiet café courtyard with a novel, walking through manicured palace gardens, and enjoying romantic quiet streets at night, book the <strong>Left Bank</strong>.
      </div>

