      <!-- Heading 8: Curated FAQ Section -->
      <h2>8. Frequently Asked Questions About Staying in Paris</h2>

      <div class="faq-accordion">
        <div class="faq-item active">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Where is the safest area to stay in Paris?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Paris is generally a very safe global metropolis, but the <strong>6th Arrondissement (Saint-Germain)</strong>, <strong>7th Arrondissement (Eiffel/Invalides)</strong>, and <strong>16th Arrondissement (Passy/Trocadéro)</strong> are among the safest, wealthiest residential neighborhoods in Europe. Violent crime is rare across central Paris; pickpocketing in crowded Metro stations and tourist clusters (around the Eiffel Tower, Sacré-Cœur, and Gare du Nord) is the primary safety concern.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How much does a hotel in central Paris cost in 2026?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>On average for central arrondissements (1st–8th): clean 3-star boutique hotels range between <strong>€160 and €260 per night</strong>; charming 4-star boutique hotels range between <strong>€280 and €450 per night</strong>; while 5-star palace hotels (like Le Meurice, Hôtel de Crillon, or Plaza Athénée) typically start at <strong>€1,100+ per night</strong>. For better value, look at high-design hotels in the 10th and 11th arrondissements (€140–€220/night).</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How many days are ideal for a first-time trip to Paris?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>We recommend a minimum of <strong>4 to 5 full days</strong>. This provides sufficient breathing room to see major landmarks (Louvre, Eiffel Tower, Notre-Dame, Orsay) without rushing, while leaving unstructured afternoons to enjoy sidewalk café culture, boutique shopping, and garden strolls.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">Is it necessary to speak French to stay in Paris?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>No, English is widely spoken in hotels, restaurants, shops, and transport hubs. However, the cultural ritual of greeting is critical: always begin any interaction with a warm <em>"Bonjour, Madame/Monsieur"</em> before asking a question or ordering. This simple courtesy dramatically transforms your customer service experience across France.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3 class="faq-question">How do I travel from Paris to Morocco?</h3>
          <div class="faq-answer">
            <p>Multiple airlines operate direct, nonstop flights between Paris (CDG and ORY) and Morocco (Marrakech RAK, Casablanca CMN, Fes FEZ, and Tangier TNG). Flight duration is approximately 3 hours and 10 minutes. Transavia, Air France, and Royal Air Maroc run several flights daily, with one-way fares often available from €60 to €180 depending on the season.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

